### What is the Smallest Missing Positive problem?

Given an unsorted array that may contain duplicates, negatives, and zero, find the smallest positive integer starting from 1 that does not appear in the array. The constraint is O(n) time and O(1) extra space, which rules out sorting (O(n log n)) and hash sets (O(n) space).

```java
package com.bank.token;

public class SmallestMissingPositive {

    public static int findSmallest(int[] tokens) {
        int n = tokens.length;

        // Phase 1: Cyclic placement
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (tokens[i] > 0 && tokens[i] <= n
                   && tokens[i] != tokens[tokens[i] - 1]) {
                int target = tokens[i] - 1;
                int temp = tokens[i];
                tokens[i] = tokens[target];
                tokens[target] = temp;
            }
        }

        // Phase 2: Scan for first mismatch
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (tokens[i] != i + 1) return i + 1;
        }
        return n + 1;
    }
}
```

### Why is the answer bounded to 1..n+1?

An array of length n can hold at most n distinct positive integers. If those are exactly 1, 2, 3, ..., n, then the smallest missing positive is n+1. If any of those is missing, the answer is less than or equal to n. Either way, the answer lies in 1 to n+1. This bounded answer is the insight that makes the in-place approach possible: you only need n slots to track which of the first n positive integers are present.

```java
package com.bank.token;

public class BoundedAnswerDemo {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        // Array [1, 2, 3]: all present, answer is 4 (n+1)
        // Array [3, 4, -1, 1]: missing 2, answer is 2
        // Array [7, 8, 9, 11]: none of 1..4 present, answer is 1
        System.out.println(findSmallest(new int[]{1, 2, 3}));       // 4
        System.out.println(findSmallest(new int[]{3, 4, -1, 1}));   // 2
        System.out.println(findSmallest(new int[]{7, 8, 9, 11}));   // 1
    }

    private static int findSmallest(int[] a) {
        int n = a.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            while (a[i] > 0 && a[i] <= n && a[i] != a[a[i] - 1]) {
                int t = a[i] - 1; int tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[t]; a[t] = tmp;
            }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (a[i] != i + 1) return i + 1;
        return n + 1;
    }
}
```

