4
2
1
```

### How does cyclic sort place values at their home index?

For each position i, if the value tokens[i] is a positive integer in the range 1 to n and it is not already at its home index (tokens[i] - 1), swap it to its home. Repeat this while loop until the value at position i is either out of range or already home. The while loop ensures that each swap puts at least one value at its correct home, so the total number of swaps across all iterations is at most n.

```java
package com.bank.token;

public class CyclicPlacementTrace {

    public static void trace() {
        int[] tokens = {3, 4, -1, 1};
        // i=0: tokens[0]=3, home index=2, swap with tokens[2]
        //      tokens = [-1, 4, 3, 1]
        //      tokens[0]=-1, out of range, stop
        // i=1: tokens[1]=4, home index=3, swap with tokens[3]
        //      tokens = [-1, 1, 3, 4]
        //      tokens[1]=1, home index=0, swap with tokens[0]
        //      tokens = [1, -1, 3, 4]
        //      tokens[1]=-1, out of range, stop
        // i=2: tokens[2]=3, home index=2, already home, stop
        // i=3: tokens[3]=4, home index=3, already home, stop
        // Result: [1, -1, 3, 4]
        // Scan: index 1 has -1 != 2, so answer is 2
    }
}
```

### What values are skipped during placement?

The placement guard `tokens[i] > 0 && tokens[i] <= n && tokens[i] != tokens[tokens[i] - 1]` skips three categories of values. Negatives and zeros are skipped because they cannot be the answer. Values greater than n are skipped because the answer is at most n+1. Duplicates where the home index already holds the same value are skipped to prevent infinite loops.

```java
package com.bank.token;

public class SkipGuardDemo {

    public static void showSkips() {
        int[] tokens = {-5, 0, 3, 3, 7, 1};
        int n = 6;
        // -5: negative, skip
        // 0: zero, skip
        // 3: in range, place at index 2
        // 3: duplicate, tokens[2] already has 3, skip
        // 7: greater than n=6, skip
        // 1: in range, place at index 0
        // After placement: [1, 0, 3, 3, 7, -5]
        // Scan: index 1 has 0 != 2, answer is 2
    }
}
```

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty array (answer is 1), an array of all negatives (answer is 1), an array with a perfect sequence from 1 to n (answer is n+1), and an array with all duplicates of the same value.

```java
package com.bank.token;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        System.out.println(findSmallest(new int[]{}));           // 1 (empty)
        System.out.println(findSmallest(new int[]{-1, -2, -3})); // 1 (all negative)
        System.out.println(findSmallest(new int[]{1, 2, 3, 4})); // 5 (perfect)
        System.out.println(findSmallest(new int[]{2, 2, 2, 2})); // 1 (all duplicates)
    }

    private static int findSmallest(int[] a) {
        int n = a.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
            while (a[i] > 0 && a[i] <= n && a[i] != a[a[i] - 1]) {
                int t = a[i] - 1; int tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[t]; a[t] = tmp;
            }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (a[i] != i + 1) return i + 1;
        return n + 1;
    }
}
```

