### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n). Each element is swapped at most once to its home index, so the total number of swaps across all while-loop iterations is at most n. The scan phase is another O(n). Space is O(1) extra because the array itself serves as the hash table.

| Approach | Time | Space | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sorting then scanning | O(n log n) | O(1) | Not optimal |
| Hash set | O(n) | O(n) | Extra space |
| Cyclic sort in-place | O(n) | O(1) | Optimal |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is not handling duplicates, which causes an infinite swap loop. If tokens[i] equals tokens[tokens[i] - 1], both positions hold the same value, and swapping them changes nothing, so the while loop never terminates.

```java
package com.bank.token;

public class CommonMistake {

    // WRONG: infinite loop on duplicates
    public static int wrongVersion(int[] a) {
        int n = a.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            // BUG: missing duplicate guard
            while (a[i] > 0 && a[i] <= n && a[i] != i + 1) {
                int t = a[i] - 1;
                int tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[t]; a[t] = tmp;
                // If a[i] == a[t], swapping changes nothing => infinite loop!
            }
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (a[i] != i + 1) return i + 1;
        return n + 1;
    }

    // CORRECT: check a[i] != a[a[i]-1] to avoid infinite loop
    public static int correctVersion(int[] a) {
        int n = a.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (a[i] > 0 && a[i] <= n && a[i] != a[a[i] - 1]) {
                int t = a[i] - 1;
                int tmp = a[i]; a[i] = a[t]; a[t] = tmp;
            }
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (a[i] != i + 1) return i + 1;
        return n + 1;
    }
}
```

### How is this used in a banking context?

At the National Bank of India, branch token dispensers maintain an in-memory pool of returned, cancelled, and duplicated token values mixed with negative placeholders. Issuing the next token means finding the smallest positive number not currently held, without allocating a separate data structure over a large pool. The cyclic sort approach files valid tokens at their home index and returns the first empty slot in linear time and constant space.

```java
package com.bank.token;

public class TokenDispenser {

    private int[] tokenPool;

    public TokenDispenser(int[] existingTokens) {
        this.tokenPool = existingTokens.clone();
    }

    public int issueNextToken() {
        return SmallestMissingPositive.findSmallest(tokenPool);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] pool = {3, 4, -1, 1};
        TokenDispenser dispenser = new TokenDispenser(pool);
        System.out.println("Next token to issue: " + dispenser.issueNextToken());
    }
}
```

